course: everyday
lesson: 20
title: Thinking about noticing
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Many animals notice danger before we do because their senses are tuned for it: dogs hear high sounds people cannot and smell far better, and hunted animals such as rabbits and deer have eyes on the sides of their heads to see almost all around.
explain: Sound bends around corners and bounces off walls, but light travels in straight lines — so you can hear a car coming round a corner before you can see it.
explain: You have two ears, one on each side; a sound from your left reaches your left ear a tiny moment sooner and a little louder, and your brain uses that difference to tell where the sound is.
explain: A sound straight in front of you or straight behind you reaches both ears at the same time, so turning your head a little helps you find it.
explain: Example: when garden birds suddenly fall silent and fly up together, one of them has often seen a cat or a hawk — wise animals, and wise people, watch the watchers.
explain: A boy in Leeds was wearing headphones and looking at the ground while crossing a car park; he did not notice a reversing car until it was nearly on him.
explain: A girl in Montana noticed a mother moose with a calf on the trail ahead and turned back; a hiker who had not noticed walked closer and was charged.
fact: Dogs can hear high-pitched sounds that people cannot hear.
fact: A dog's sense of smell is many thousands of times more sensitive than a person's.
fact: Hunted animals like rabbits and deer often have eyes on the sides of their heads, so they can see almost all around.
fact: Hunters such as cats and owls have eyes at the front, so their two eyes see largely the same view, which helps judge distance.
fact: Owls and cats see very well in dim light, but no eye can see anything in total darkness.
fact: Eyes are especially good at noticing movement, so hunters creep slowly and prey animals often freeze.
fact: Sound waves bend around corners and bounce off walls; light travels in straight lines.
fact: A sound from one side reaches the nearer ear a tiny bit earlier and louder than the farther ear.
fact: The brain compares the two ears to work out where a sound comes from.
fact: Sounds straight in front of or behind you are harder to place, so turning your head helps.
fact: Electric cars and bikes can be very quiet at low speeds, so you must look as well as listen.
fact: Birds give alarm calls or fall silent when a predator is near, and other animals react to them.
fact: An echo is sound bounced back from a surface, and echoes can make a sound seem to come from the wrong place.
fact: People who are not paying attention notice danger too late to get out of the way.
fact: Noticing early gives you time to choose: step back, go round or get help.
exercise: Why can you hear a car coming round a corner before you can see it? | Sound bends around corners, but light travels straight | Light slows down when it goes round corners | Cars make their noise well before they start to move off | Your ears sit higher on your head than your eyes
exercise: A sound comes from your left. How does your brain know? | It reaches your left ear sooner and louder | Only your left ear can hear it at all | Sounds from the left are always higher in pitch than others | Your eyes tell your ears where to listen
exercise: Why is it hard to tell whether a sound is straight ahead or straight behind you? | It reaches both ears at the same moment | Sounds from behind you are always silent | Your ears can only face backwards | Sound cannot travel round your head
exercise: What helps you find a sound that seems to be straight ahead or behind? | Turning your head a little | Closing one of your eyes tightly | Holding your breath and keeping still | Covering both ears with your hands
exercise: Why does a dog often notice a visitor before the family does? | It hears and smells far better than people | It can see straight through the walls of the house | It knows the visitor's plans in advance | It feels the doorbell before it rings
exercise: Why do rabbits have eyes on the sides of their heads? | To see almost all around for danger | To see much better in the dark | To judge distances very exactly when jumping | To see more colours than hunters
exercise: Why do cats and owls have eyes at the front of their heads? | Their two views overlap, which helps judge distance | It lets them see behind them while they creep up on prey | It keeps their eyes warm on cold nights | It lets them see a wider circle around them
exercise: Garden birds suddenly stop singing and fly off together. What is a sensible guess? | One of them may have spotted a cat or a hawk | They have all become tired at exactly the same time | It means the day will be sunny and warm | They are flying off to find better music
exercise: Why must you look as well as listen when crossing near electric cars? | They can be very quiet at low speed | They always drive on the wrong side of the road | They are hard to see in bright sunshine | Their drivers cannot see people walking
exercise: In a narrow street between tall buildings, a siren seems to come from the wrong direction. Why? | Echoes bounce the sound off the walls | The siren is moving backwards | Sound travels faster between walls | Tall buildings block all of the sound
exercise: Why do animals in the wild often seem to «know» about danger first? | Their senses pick up small signs that we miss | They can read the thoughts of people around them | They are warned by signals from the sky | They can always tell the future
exercise: A boy covers his right ear and listens for a whistle. Why is it now harder to tell where it is? | His brain cannot compare the two ears | The whistle gets much louder on the right | Sound only goes in the left ear anyway | His eyes work less with one ear covered
exercise: Why is it useful to notice what animals around you are doing? | Their reactions can warn you of things you missed | Animals always know when the buses come | Animals can tell you the exact time | Animals only ever react to real danger, never to anything else
exercise: Why does a hunting cat creep low and very slowly? | Slow movement is harder for prey to notice | It is too tired to run any faster than that | Moving slowly makes it invisible | It is waiting for the prey to fall fast asleep first
test: Standing at a street corner, you hear a bike bell but see no bike. Which is true? | The bike may be round the corner, since sound bends | The bike must be right beside you | The sound is coming from the sky | There is no bike, it is only your imagination
test: If people had only one ear, what would become harder? | Telling which direction a sound comes from | Hearing loud sounds at all | Hearing any high sounds, like birds singing in the trees | Speaking clearly to others
test: A deer and a fox stand in a field. Which one sees more of what is behind it without turning? | The deer, with eyes on the sides of its head | The fox, with eyes at the front of its head | Both see exactly the same amount | Neither can see anything behind at all
test: Your dog barks at the front door, though you heard nothing. What is the best explanation? | The dog heard or smelled something you could not | The dog is barking at nothing at all | The dog can see right through the door with its sharp eyes | The dog is able to predict the future
test: In a long tunnel, a train sounds as if it is coming from both ends. Why? | Echoes carry the sound along the walls | Sound cannot travel inside tunnels at all | There are always two trains at once | Tunnels make sound move backwards
test: A girl hears a beep but cannot tell if it is in front of her or behind her. What should she do? | Turn her head and listen again | Stand perfectly still and wait for it | Cover both ears at the same time | Shut her eyes as tightly as she can
test: Why is a quiet electric scooter especially risky for a walker staring at a phone with headphones on? | Neither sound nor sight would warn the walker | Electric scooters always go faster than cars | Headphones attract electric scooters | Electric scooters have no brakes at all
test: A whistle blows on your right. Which ear hears it first? | The right ear, a tiny bit earlier | The left ear, a tiny bit earlier | Both at exactly the same moment | Neither, until you turn around
test: Why does a fox often freeze when a rabbit lifts its head? | The rabbit notices movement more than still shapes | Freezing makes the fox turn invisible | The fox has stopped to listen to the birds singing nearby | A fox must rest after every few steps
test: why do rabbits have eyes on the sides | To see almost all around for danger | To look cuter | To see in the dark like owls and cats do | To smell better
test: why's the cat creeping so slow at the bird | Slow movement is hard to notice | Cats are always tired after a long sleep | To keep its soft paws nice and clean | Because birds cannot hear it at all
test: dog barks at nothing, i can't hear anything | Dogs hear high sounds we can't | Dogs just bark for no reason at all | It's seeing ghosts in the hallway | Its ears are broken or something
test: electric car came right up behind me, didnt hear it | Look as well as listen, they're quiet | Electric cars are always loud, so you'd hear it | Only listen, never look | They only drive at night
test: why does mum say look around u in car parks | Drivers reversing may not see you, so you must see them | Car parks are boring otherwise | Cars only ever drive forwards there, never backwards | You might find money on the ground
test: trail's got a big animal ahead, it hasn't seen us | Back away quietly and go another way | Walk closer slowly for a really good photo | Shout so it runs away | Throw food to make it friendly
test: Emma says noticing stuff is just for spies. true? | No, it keeps everyone safe every day | Yes, normal people do not need it | Yes, only police notice things | Yes, unless you are lost
test: they reckon an electric car ran over a kid's foot in the car park cos nobody heard it coming | Real lesson: electric cars can be silent at low speed, so look as well as listen | Real lesson: electric cars are always louder than others, so the kid wasn't listening | It proves car parks are only dangerous for adults | It is gossip, so listening is always enough
test: my cousin says owls can see in total pitch dark like night vision goggles | That is an exaggeration; owls see well in dim light but no eye can see in total darkness | He is right, owls see in total darkness | He is right, and so can cats and dogs | He is right, but only with one eye
test: someone says Theo's deaf in one ear cos he's weird and that's why he doesn't answer, pass it on | Do not pass it on; it is cruel, and if he is hard of hearing he deserves kindness | Pass it on, so people know why he is weird | Pass it on, because it explains everything | Pass it on, but only to the teacher
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the position of the eyes of hunted animals such as rabbits and deer? | The eyes are set upon the sides of the head, affording a view of almost all the surroundings | The eyes are set at the front of the head for the judging of distance | The eyes are set upon the top of the head for the observation of birds | The eyes are positioned identically to those of an owl
exam: Identify the means by which the brain determines the direction from which a sound originates. | By comparing the sound as it arrives at the two ears | By measuring the colour of the sound | By relying solely upon the sense of smell | By detecting the vibration of the floor beneath the feet
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the travel of sound and light. | Sound bends about corners and is reflected by walls, whereas light travels in straight lines | Light bends about corners, whereas sound travels only in straight lines | Neither sound nor light can be reflected by walls | Sound and light travel at precisely the same speed
exam: It being given that a small bird gives an alarm call and then falls silent, determine what such conduct most probably signifies. | That a predator is near | That rain shall shortly fall | That the bird has found food | That the day is concluding
